[Music]  a 20 year old petrol station worker was  shot dead in germany after asking a  customer to mask up  [Music]  the country has seen many protests  sometimes violent over its hygiene rules  but the shooting was the first death  directly linked to the restrictions  it prompted widespread shock and  condemnation  only because we have measures that we're  supposed to follow  it's an escalation of conspiracy  theories  how can this radicalization be stopped  welcome to our covet 19 special today  focusing on how the pandemic fuels anger  among coronavirus skeptics right up to  the point of physical violence a  phenomenon all over the world  most demonstrations against coronavirus  regulations are peaceful like here in  slovenia but even this protest saw  pockets of violence  [Music]  in melbourne australia it was a  different matter  protests in mid-september escalated with  demonstrators targeting journalists  crowd is certainly getting bigger  they're very passionate  i've just been struck in the back of a  backpack of a head by a can  that really hurt  i've been i've been grabbed around the  neck today  i've had urine tipped on me  now i've had a can of an energy drink a  throne on me  just moments before protesters pushed  paul dowsley and his camera team onto  the pavement and threatened them the  organization reporters without borders  says such acts have become more common  everywhere since the pandemic started  including in germany  we're dealing with a new kind of  extremism here it's no longer just  left-wing and right-wing extremism and  islamism but a kind of conspiracy  extremism i'm almost inclined to call it  coronavirus extremism because it really  is an ideology of its own that's  establishing itself and is now  displaying violent traits and is acting  out violence  an ideology with a recurring theme  conspiracy theories  they say face masks are nothing less  than muzzles  that vaccinations will kill us  and the virus is an invention of the  powerful to repress the people  such beliefs make it difficult to get  covert 19 under control  and those subscribing to them can lose  all sense of proportion  there are public calls for murder and  revolution  so how can societies get radicalization  under control  well that is something i'd like to  explore now with uh pia lamberty and  peter knight pia is a social  psychologist with a special focus on  conspiracy theories she's written books  and articles on this topic  and peter is a professor of american  studies at the university of manchester  which also co-organizes the infodemic  project studying the origin of  conspiracy theories and how to combat  them good to have both of you with us  and i'd like to start with you pia and  your assessment of what happened here in  germany a young man being shot pointing  for pointing out the mask requirement to  a customer who then said he wanted to  set an example against the coronavirus  restrictions is this just an isolated  case some individual gone mad or do you  see a trend  um i wouldn't say it's an isolated  incident because there  yeah reason why the person shot another  man was because he said the mask was  um yeah a representation of supposed  suppression of  free speech and so on and this is  something we see through the whole  pandemic that the mass is politicalized  by populist parties that they use it as  a symbol to yeah portray themselves as  heroes and we see that there's a lot of  violence in the german society we have  so many incidents where people  are beaten up because of the face mass  we have arsenal attacks against  scientific institutions against vaccine  centers  against trains and so i think if you  frame it as a single incident that's  wrong and you ignore the violence that  we see since almost two years now  and peter i mean i know that there were  corona protests in the uk also in the  united states do you observe a similar  level of radicalization there  yeah just in the last week we've seen a  number of incidents for example  a man in maryland  allegedly killed his  brother and sister-in-law because his  brother was a pharmacist handing out  coded  shots vaccination shots in the uk there  have been online a lot of calls for a  second nuremberg trial  to  arrest and execute journalists and  doctors  so it can seem that  corona protests have become increasingly  radicalized but i think we need to  view this slightly more  skeptically vaccine refusal across the  eu  is at most  10 perhaps in many countries only 5  so we're talking about a small number of  people  who are taking these more extreme views  and certainly in terms of people engaged  in acts of extreme violence they are  still very very much in the minority in  a way that people  who are attracted to forms of right-wing  extremism  online  but it's it's all of this is all of this  down to misinformation as the who for  example uh  suggests or is there more to it why this  radicalization peter  it's not a lack of information when it  comes to conspiracy theories conspiracy  theorists  have endless access  to to information  instead i think we need to think that  conspiracy theorists are attracted to an  entire worldview a sense of identity and  no amount of fact-checking or kind of  information supply is going to change  their mind  okay so not even uh something that would  actually give them what they want appear  we've seen in the united kingdom a few  months ago uh the so-called freedom day  all the mask rules all those measures  were dropped  do you think would a similar move here  in germany eventually appease radical  corona deniers or would they find  something else to rant on about  i don't think that this will change  their mind when we talk about these  people with a strong ideology it's not  gone just because the measures are gone  we also see when we have a look at um  yeah the main  ideologists behind this  they are already claiming climate change  is the new topic and when we had the bad  floodings in germany they were spreading  a lot of conspiracy theories about  climate change so i guess these networks  are there  we can say that we have around about 200  000 people that are in these telegram  groups and channels um and at the whole  time confronted with these kind of  conspiracy theories and one problem is  they are not the majority of course but  they try to portray themselves as if  they are speaking for the people and if  they are the majority and in germany  they are very professional in doing so  and for example in berlin now they  changed the law that kids don't have to  wear a face mask anymore in school and  the reason was because people wrote so  many emails  that they don't want that anymore and  this has to do with the mobilization  from the spectrum we can see that in our  monitoring  all right pierre lamberty there and  peter knight  thank you so much for sharing your  expertise with us  there's so much more to explore maybe we  have another uh chance uh another time  because i'm afraid that uh topic will  not go away anytime soon so pia lamberty  peter knight thank you very much  thank you thank you  time now for one of your questions  here's our science correspondent derek  williams  [Music]  what role does social media play in a  lack of enthusiasm to get vaccinated  this is another question that's kind of  more socio-political that than  scientific but science does have  something to say about it and i'll get  back to that in a minute um first though  let's think about social media  seen from its most positive side it's  fantastic um digital platforms allow us  to share our thoughts and our beliefs  with our family and our friends and our  colleagues and the whole planet if we  want to um directly  instantly unfiltered they they let us  easily  create and and join communities which is  great  but that's also a double-edged sword  because researchers have shown pretty  definitively  that when it comes to big questions like  whether or not to get vaccinated  most of us listen first to what our  communities are saying  even if they disagree with expert advice  so if you're involved in communities  online where where pseudoscience and  misinformation  is widespread  then it'll probably have a big impact on  what you eventually end up believing  now the science um there are plenty of  studies looking at how big of a role  social media plays in vaccine acceptance  or vaccine hesitancy an interesting one  conducted in the u.s  back in december of last year for  instance  so right around the time that vaccines  were first being rolled out and people  were were understandably wary  it revealed  a fascinating split  its results indicated that people who  got some  or most of their information from  traditional media like newspapers  rather than social media that they were  also the people  most likely to accept vaccines the  authors concluded  that that's because traditional media  providers in general  rely more on data-driven content  and other studies have come to similar  conclusions so  so while social media brings us together  and has revolutionized how we interact  with each other in lots of positive ways  in many places it's also strengthened  the echo chamber effect and that  contributes in a big way to to division  on questions like  whether to get vaccinated or not  [Music]  more food for thought there and that  brings us to the end of this edition of  our kobit 19 special for me and the team  thanks for watching  [Music]

